data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core for .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider deprecated synchronous operations but allowed a **sync-over-async opt-in** (e.g., `EnableSyncOverAsync()`). In .NET 11, **that opt-in is removed entirely**. Every synchronous LINQ operator and `SaveChanges()` call against the Cosmos provider now throws an `InvalidOperationException` at runtime — there is no workaround other than going async.

### All four methods break:

| Method | Throws on |
|---|---|
| `GetAll()` | `.ToList()` |
| `GetById()` | `.First(...)` |
| `GetCount()` | `.Count()` |
| `Save()` | `.SaveChanges()` |

### Fix: convert to async equivalents

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `Add()` is purely in-memory (change tracker), so it stays synchronous — only the I/O call (`SaveChangesAsync`) needs the async form.
- All callers up the chain must also become `async`/`await` (this "infects" up to the controller/endpoint level, which is the desired pattern for Cosmos DB's fully async SDK).
- Make sure you're importing `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore` for the async extension methods (`ToListAsync`, `FirstAsync`, `CountAsync`).
